  "text_md": "POCHERS. Potters?\nNorth ;\n- (2) A clump of trees. North.\nPLUFFY. Spongy\nPLUMPY. To churn. Comw.\nPLUNGE. (1) A deep pool. Somerset.\n; heavy. East.\ntogether. West.\nPLOWSHO. A ploughshare. Kennett.\nPOBS. Porridge. Craven.\n- PLUG. A dwarfish fellow. East. PLUM. (1) Light ; soft. West.\nA pruest proud ase a po,\nForhyme that is smetyne with his awenne blode, and spredis over alle his lymmes, and waxes plowkky, and brekes owte. MS. Lincoln Med. f. 294.\n- (3) A crowd of people ; a mass of anything. It is sometimes a verb, to collect together.\nPOARE-BLIND. Dim-sighted. The word\nPLOYE. A plough. NominaleMS.\nPLOWRING. Weeping. Prompt. Parv.\nPLUMAKIN. The magnum-bonum plum.\n- (4) A pump ; a draw-well. Comw.\nTo see, and drink a little pluck.\nWright's Political Songs, p. 159.\n3forte Arthui-e, MS. Lincoln, f. 76.\nterms of ancient alchemy.\nPLOWEFERE. Companion in play. (J.-S.)\n(2) A strait or difficulty. Greene. PLUNGY. Wet; rainy. {A.-N) PLUNKET. A coarse woollen cloth. PLUNKY. Short ; thick\n- (6) Same as (1) ? I had the luck\nbecause we use a staff in cuerpo, but not when we wear a cloak.\"\nPO. A peacock. {A.-S.)\nPLUFE. A plough. Yorksh.\n- (5) Plum round, quite round. \" Make their attire to sit plum round,\" Harrison, p. 172. Plumfat, Florio, p. 33.\n- (6) Directly ; exactly. Var. dial. Forby has plumpendicular, perpendicular.\nPLURISY. Superabundance. Shak.\nperpendicular. Plum\n- (4) Straight ; upright\nPLUCKING. The worsted plucked from the\n![](<11_pg161_pg1_images/imageFile2.png>)\nPLUMMY. Soft ; wet ; mouldy. Devon. PLUMP. (1) Dry ; hard. Kent.\nPLUCK. (1) Courage. Var. dial. \"To pluck up one's heart,\" to be bold, to rejoice. Against\nhas a large knob. Glouc.\nPLOW-LODE. \" Caracuta, plow lode,\" Nominale MS. It seems to be the same as\nBrome'a Songs, 1661, p. 167.\nPLYER. A very common bawd. PLYMOUTH-CLOAK. A cane, or stick. So\nAnd they shote plucke buffet,\nForsoth as I you say,\n- (7) A student who fails in an university examination is said to be plucked.\nPOAD-MILK. The first milk given by cows\nPLUM-TREE. The female pudendum. Have\noccurs in Hollyband's Dictionarie, 1593. POAT. To kick. Devon.\nPLUME. To pick or pluck the feathers off a\nPOACHING. Swampy. Devon.\nmachine while the wheel is turning. North.\nPLUERE. Weeping. {J.-N.)\n- (2) To pluck a crow or goose with any one, i. e. to quarrel with him.\nPLOWKKY. Covered with pimples.",
